# Line endings, declared rather than left to each machine's `core.autocrlf`.
#
# Without this file the repository checked out CRLF on Windows and LF on the Linux CI
# runner. That is invisible until something reads source as text: three tests assert
# properties of the source they are checking, and one of them compared a whole line, so
# it passed on every pull request and failed on the machine it was written on, printing
# expected and received values that looked identical. The tests normalize on read, and
# this says what the files are so the next one does not have to.
#
# `eol=lf` rather than `text=auto` alone: the latter normalizes the repository but still
# hands Windows a CRLF working tree, which is the half that caused the problem.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, never touched.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.lib binary
*.snap text

# Windows-only scripts that genuinely want CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
